  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nQuiet and near seasonable conditions persist early this afternoon. \nStratus from this morning has dissipated across the area, allowing \nhigh temperatures to warm to the 20s and 30s. High level cirrus will \ncontinue to push in aloft ahead of an incoming shortwave trough. \nThis will allow for cloud cover to thicken through the evening and \novernight hours. While a cold surface high will push through the \narea tonight, have bumped up low temperatures just a bit as cloud \ncover should limit nocturnal cooling just a little bit. Still have \nlows bottoming out in the teens and 20s, coldest east of I-29.\n\nTuesday will be a cloudy day as the next chance for precipitation, \nin the form of snow, will make a return to the area. Tuesday morning \nlooks to remain dry but could see flurries, and maybe a few \nsprinkles near the James River, by early Tuesday afternoon. Could \nsee some light snow slowly work its way into the area from the north \nas the snow fights through low level dry air. Highs look to warm to \njust a bit above freezing as snow begins to fall, mainly for \nlocations east of the James River. Areas along and west of the James \nwill warm to the upper 30s to low 40s. With temperatures largely \nremaining around to below freezing, road temperatures look to remain \nat freezing or colder, allowing for snow to stick to the roads \nquickly. That said, this snow will be driven by warm air advection \n(WAA). The WAA being the main forcing mechanism looks to deliver a \nsomewhat longer stretch of light snow, especially as the snow looks \nto expand in coverage Tuesday evening. The snow will push to the \neast during the evening into the overnight hours, mainly occurring \nalong and east of I-29. Should see the snow wind down Wednesday \nmorning. Snowfall amounts have continued to trend up slightly, now \nexpecting between a quarter of an inch to up to an inch of snow east \nof the James River. The highest snowfall totals look to fall along \nthe Buffalo Ridge in southwest Minnesota and adjacent ares in \nnorthwest Iowa. This is also the same area where isolated higher \ntotals could occur, potentially as high as 2 inches. Despite how \nlight the total snowfall will be, this is the first time we are \ngetting snow this season. With snow falling mainly during the night \ntime hours later Tuesday into Wednesday morning, minor travel \nimpacts are possible. If you are planning on traveling for work, \nschool, or holiday plans, make sure to take it slow and drive to \nconditions. \n\nHigh temperatures look to warm above freezing by Wednesday \nafternoon, though this could be mitigated by how much snow does \nfall. Should highs remain near to below freezing (<= 20% chance), \nthen the snow will not melt much, if not at all. This could still \nresult in some travel impacts depending on how much snow remains on \nany untreated roadways. \n\nThe rest of the week and weekend looks quiet as a moisture \nstarved systems slides through the Northern Plains. Not \nimpossible to see the models pick up on some light precipitation\nas the end of the week and weekend draws closer. That said, \nconfidence remains too low to deviate from the NBM. Temperatures\nwill warm through the rest of the week, back into the 40s to \neven 50s on Friday before slightly cooling back to the 40s over \nthe weekend. Breezy west/northwest winds are possible during \nthis period of time, which could result in wind chills down to \nnear to below freezing.",
